      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we studied how the development of counting systems has helped shape our daily lives. Now, let us explore another skill essential to both everyday life and science: the act of comparing.\n\nThink about it for a moment:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Comparing</b><br /><br />Are two red apples the same?<br /><br />What about two cars of the same model but different colors? </div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Often, we classify things as equal because they look alike, or we see them as completely different when they actually share many traits. In this chapter, we will examine these contradictions and deepen our understanding of what it truly means to compare.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_initial",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>What Does It Mean to Be Equal?</h3>Before we begin comparing, it is important to define the concept of equality. For this study, we highlight the following definition of the word equal from the Britannica Dictionary (<htcite0>):</p><p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li>\"<i>B1. the same in amount, number, or size.</i>\"</li></ul></p><p>With this definition in mind, let us examine <b>Chart 1</b>:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Two identical circles.</p><p>In <b>Chart 1</b>, we see two circles that perfectly match the definition of equal—they are identical in every characteristic. Now, let's compare this with <b>Chart 2</b>:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh; background-color: blue;\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Two similar circles, but with different colors.</p><p>In <b>Chart 2</b>, the two circles are identical in every way except for their color. Thus, while they are similar (<htcite1>), they are not exactly the same.</p><p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: On Groups</b><br /><br />Does changing the color of an object change its type?</div></div></center></p><p>To answer this question, we first need to define what a circle is. According to Eric W. Weisstein definition (<htcite2>):</p><p><div class=\"desc\"><div class=\"circle2\" style=\"width:12vh;height:12vh;display: flex;align-items: center;justify-content: center;\"><div class=\"circle\" style=\"width:1vh;height:1vh;background-color:black\"></div></div></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: A circle used in the mathematical definition.</p><p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li>\"<i>A circle is the set of points in a plane that are equidistant from a given central point.</i>\"</li></ul></p><p>Since the definition of a circle does not include color as an essential characteristic, the fact that the circles in <b>Chart 2</b> have different colors does not alter their geometric properties. They remain circles.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "425c72c4-6286-44cf-80e9-d9b21d0735ea",
                     "text": "The Britannica Dictionary",
